  "category": "hedge",
  "note": "Source \"should\" strengthened to \"must\" (twice) and the Deus ex Machina \"should be employed only\" weakened to a flat statement.",
  "replacement": "The poet should always aim at the necessary or the probable — in drawing character just as in building the structure of the plot. A person of a given character should speak or act in a given way because necessity or probability requires it, and one event should follow another by the same necessary or probable sequence. It is therefore evident that the unraveling of a plot, like the complication, has to grow out of the plot itself. The 'Deus ex Machina' must not bring it about — as happens in the Medea, or in the Return of the Greeks in the Iliad. The 'Deus ex Machina' should be used only for events outside the drama: those that happened before it or will happen after it, which no human being could know and which therefore have to be reported or foretold, since we credit the gods with seeing all things. Nothing irrational may occur within the action. If the irrational cannot be avoided, it should stay outside the scope of the tragedy. The irrational element in the Oedipus of Sophocles is of this kind."
 },
